## A B S T R A C T
Keywords: Banana cellulose nanofibres Amphiphilic polymer Zn-metal-organic framework
In this study, banana cellulose nanofibres (BNCFs) were prepared using waste banana stems. A novel twodimensional (2D) $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF was synthesized using 5-(1-hydro-imidazolyl) isophthalic acid as the ligand. The re­ sults of the X-ray diffraction, thermogravimetry, and scanning electron microscopy analyses confirmed the stability of Zn-MOF. Thus, Zn-MOF was used to catalyze BNCFs grafted with poly(ε-caprolactone) (ε-CL) (BGCL) by homogeneous ring-opening polymerization in ionic liquid. The grafting ratio of the BGCL prepared by Zn-MOF reached $84.12~\%$ , which was $10.03\%$ higher than that catalyzed by 4-dimethylamino pyridine which was the state-of-the-art catalyst for this reaction. The banana cellulose nanofibres grafted with ε-CL reactions followed the coordination-insertion mechanism, which was confirmed by the theoretical calculations using the Materials Studio software. The cytotoxicity results indicated that BGCL was not only non-toxic to Hela cells but also promoted Hela cell growth within a certain concentration range. Therefore, this study demonstrated the promising potential of BGCL for biomedical applications, and the high efficiency, non-toxicity, and stability of Zn-MOF as the catalyst. Further, this will provide a theoretical basis for the application of MOFs as catalysts in polymerization reactions.
## 1. Introduction
Amphiphilic polymers have important applications in emerging high-technology fields, such as drug delivery, bioimaging, sensing, and tissue engineering scaffolds [1–3]. However, the high cost and potential biotoxicity of synthetic polymers have limited their further application as biomaterials. Recently, cellulose-based amphiphilic polymers have garnered a growing interest owing to their advantages of abundance, recyclability, non-toxic degradation, easy absorption, and low inflam­ mation; thus, they have good biological activities as anti-cancer, antiacidic, and anti-coagulation materials, among others, resulting in wide application prospects in the biomedical field [1].
Banana is a popular fruit that grows in Guangxi Province, China. Commonly, people only eat the pulp of the banana, whereas its stems and leaves, which are cellulose sources, are discarded. The use of banana stems and leaves not only adds value to a waste product but also protects the environment; banana fibres are natural fibres with the advantages of abundant source, renewability, low price, and degradability [4–9]. Owing to their low environmental impact, biocompatibility, and hy­ drophilicity, cellulose nanofibres have potential applications in the fields of biomaterials, drug delivery, and tissue engineering scaffold [10]. Polycaprolactone (PCL) exhibits admirable biocompatibility and biodegradability and is usually utilized in tissue engineering scaffolds, and drug carriers [1,11–14]. However, the low mechanical strength, strong hydrophobicity, and poor cell affinity of PCL limit their further application.
Previous studies indicate that stannous octanoate $\left[\mathsf{S n}(\mathsf{O c t})_{2}\right]$ and 4- dimethylamino pyridine (DMAP) have a catalytic effect on the reac­ tion of cellulose-grafted ε-caprolactone (ε-CL) [1,2]. These common reactions are divided into two types, namely homogeneous and heterogeneous reactions. Heterogeneous reactions have been reported in previous studies [15–18]. Heterogeneous catalysts have the advan­ tages of easy separation from the reaction system after the reaction and recyclability; however, these catalysts have low catalytic efficiency. But cellulose is difficult to disperse uniformly in common solvents, such as water [19,20]. Huang et al. confirmed that many catalytic reactions occur inside the pores, MCC was difficult to enter the narrow pores of catalysts due to its large particles and high crystallinity [20]. Currently, novel cellulose-dissolving systems are garnering increasing attention, because they can overcome the above-mentioned limitations. Cellulosebased amphiphiles are prepared in homogenous solutions [1,2]. For cellulose-grafted ε-CL with tin catalysts, the release of tin ions could have adverse long-term effects [21]. Moreover, the high toxicity of DMAP prompts the need for the application of less harmful catalysts.
MOFs as potential catalysts have garnered the interest of researchers owing to their high internal surface areas (up to $7000\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ , tuneable pore shape and size, and high densities of catalytic sites [22]. Moreover, the high porosity and significant physical and chemical properties of MOFs indicate their good catalytic properties. Currently, the application of MOFs in Knoevenagel condensation [23], Friedel-Crafts reactions [24], oxidation reactions [25], and other reactions have been widely investigated. Moreover, MOFs as catalysts are mostly reported in anaerobic compounds and inorganic gas [22]. However, the application of MOFs in polymerization reactions is still rarely reported. Most studies use MOFs as templates for polymerization reactions to control the microstructure [26] and macroscopic properties [27] (e.g. size and mechanical properties) of polymerized products. Limited studies have focused on the thermal stability and regeneration of MOFs that make them potential catalysts in polymerization reactions.
In this study, banana fibres were extracted by the alkaline boiling of the waste banana stems at Nanning City, Guangxi Province, China. Ba­ nana cellulose microcrystals were obtained by pretreatment and alkali treatment. Banana cellulose nanofibres (BNCFs) were prepared by a cation exchange resin method. Two-dimensional (2D) Zn-MOF was synthesized using 5-(1-hydro-imidazolyl) isophthalic acid $\mathrm{(H_{2}L)}$ as the ligand with $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ through a hydrothermal process. Zn-MOF was used to catalyze the BNCFs grafted with ε-CL (BGCL). The effects of the ratio of BNCFs to ε-CL ([AGU]/[CL]), catalyst content, reaction temperature, and reaction time were investigated. The catalytic prop­ erties of DMAP and Zn-MOF were compared under the same synthesis conditions. Moreover, the crystallization properties, thermal stability, surface morphology, and cytotoxicity of BGCL were examined. The re­ action mechanism of the catalysts was studied. Overall, we demon­ strated the improved catalytic effect of Zn-MOF on BGCL than that of DMAP. Moreover, aside from the non-toxicity of BGCL, it was found to promote Hela cell growth within a certain concentration range.
## 2. Experimental section
## 2.1. Materials
The banana fibres were obtained from the waste banana stems around Nanning City, Guangxi Province, China. BNCFs were prepared from the banana fibres using a chemical pretreatment method [28–30]; ε-CL with a purity of $99\%$ was obtained from Aladdin Reagent Inc. As ionic liquids, 1-allyl-3-methylimidazolium chloride ([Amim]Cl; purity of $99.0~\%$ ) was supplied by Lanzhou Institute of Chemical Physics, Chinese Academy of Science, and 5-(1-hydro-imidazolyl) isophthalic acid was purchased by Jinan Henghua Sci. & Tec. Co. Ltd.
## 2.2. Preparation of the BNCFs, Zn-MOF, and BGCL
2.2.1. Preparation of the banana cellulose microfibres and BNCFs The banana fibres were extracted from the banana stems through acid soaking and alkali treatment, $0.2\mathrm{wt}\%\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4}$ was used to soak the banana stems for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ , and a mixed solution of $6~\mathrm{g/L}$ NaOH, $_{0.6\ \mathrm{g/L}}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}S{\mathrm O}_{3},$ and $\mathrm{Na_{4}P_{2}O_{7}{\cdot}10H_{2}O}$ were used to alkali boiling for $2\mathrm{h}.2\mathrm{g}/\mathrm{L}$ NaOH and $1.5~\mathrm{g/L}$ soap were used to simmer for $^{2\mathrm{~h~}}$ . acidolysis was successively implemented to prepare banana cellulose microfibres, $_{2g}$ banana cellulose microfibres, $_{16\mathrm{~g~}}$ cation exchange resin, and $250~\mathrm{mL}$ $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ were added to the three-neck flask and kept at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under mag­ netic stirring for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ to prepare the BNCFs, the detailed steps using the methodology described by previous studies [28–30]. The BNCFs were characterized by transmission electron microscopy (FEI Tecnai G2 20) and dynamic light scattering ((HOROBI Instruments pte Ltd., SZ-100, Singapore) Fig. S1).
## 2.2.2. Synthesis of Zn-MOF
A mixture of $\mathrm{H_{2}L}$ ( $\mathrm{0.25}\mathrm{mmol}$ , $0.058{\mathrm{g}}{\cdot}$ , $\mathrm{Zn}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}{\cdot}2\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ 1 $\mathrm{0.15\:mmol}$ , $0.033{\mathrm{g}})$ , DMF $(2\mathrm{mL})$ , $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ ( ${\mathrm{.9~mL}}$ , and KOH $\mathrm{58}\mathrm{mg})$ was placed in a 15 mL Teflon-lined stainless steel container, stirred for $30\mathrm{min}$ , and heated at $170^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 3 days. After cooling to $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , white needle crystals were obtained at a yield of $41.05~\%$ . These needle crystals were heated in a tube furnace at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $14\mathrm{~h~}$ under vacuum $(0.09\mathrm{MPa})$ . The Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra of the $Z\mathbf{n}$ -MOF prepared in KBr discs were recorded in the range $400{-}4000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ on a Nicolet iS10 spec­ trometer, The data (Fig. S2) are as follows: 3389(s), 3143(w), 1631(m), 1485(m), 1281(m), 1263(m), 1208(m), 1073(s), 986(s). The CCDC number is 2154913.
## 2.2.3. Synthesis of BGCL
## 2.3. Characterization of Zn-MOF and BGCL
Single-crystal X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were recorded on a Rigaku OD SuperNova Single source diffractometer with an Eos detector and graphite-monochromatized Mo Kα radiation $(\lambda=0.71073\mathring\mathrm{A})$ at 273 K. The crystal structures were analyzed using SHELXT (2015) [32] and refined by SHELXL-2015 [33] within Olex2 (2009) [34]. The $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms were determined using a specific surface and aperture analyser (3H-2000PS1, Bei Shi de Instrument Technology Co. Ltd., Beijing, P.R. China) at 77 K; the temperature was controlled using a liquid nitrogen bath. The analysis was based on the Bru­ nauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) adsorption isotherm. Solvent-free Zn-MOF was also obtained at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $14\mathrm{~h~}$ under vacuum $(0.09\mathrm{\MPa})$ . The Fourier transform infrared (FT-IR) spectra of the BNCFs and Zn-MOF prepared in KBr discs were recorded in the range $400{-}4000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ on a Nicolet iS10 spectrometer in the attenuated total reflectance mode. The $^1\mathrm{H}$ nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) and $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR spectra of the BGCL were recorded on an NMR spectrometer (Bruker, Avance 400, Germany) with tetramethylsilane as the internal standard and dimethylsulfoxideD6 ( ${\bf\dot{d}}_{6}$ -DMSO) as the solvent. The PXRD experiments were determined by an X-ray diffractometer (Bruker, D8 Advance, Germany) with $\mathtt{C u K}\alpha$ radiation $\overset{\cdot}{(\lambda}=1.54056\overset{\circ}{\mathrm{A}})$ in the range of $5\mathrm{-}50^{\circ}$ at a speed of $10^{\circ}/\mathrm{min}$ with an X-ray tube voltage and current of $40\mathrm{kV}$ and $25\mathrm{mA}$ , respectively, at room temperature. The thermogravimetric analysis (TGA; TA, Dis­ covery, USA) was carried out from room temperature to $800^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with a nitrogen flow of $50\ \mathrm{mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ at a heating rate of $20\ ^{\circ}\mathbf{C}{\cdot}\mathbf{min}^{-1}$ . The samples $(4{-}6~\mathrm{mg})$ were heated. The differential scanning calorimetry analysis (TA, Q20, USA) was carried out with a nitrogen flow of 50 $\mathrm{\mL}{\cdot}\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ at a heating rate of $10\ {^{\circ}\mathbf{C}}{\cdot}\mathbf{min}^{-1}$ . The samples $(4{-}6\mathrm{mg})$ were heated. The microstructure and morphology properties of Zn-MOF were observed by scanning electron microscopy (SEM, Hitachi, FlexSEM1000 II, Japan). BGCL was observed after gold spraying by a field emission SEM (FE-SEM, Hitachi, S-4800, Japan).
## 2.4. In vitro cytotoxicity
The cytotoxicity of BGCL $(\mathrm{Zn-MOF})$ and BGCL (DMAP) was measured using Hela cells. Hela cell suspensions ${\mathrm{100~}}\upmu\mathrm{L},$ /well) were inoculated in a 96-well plate and incubated overnight. The BGCL (Zn-MOF) and BGCL (DMAP) were dissolved using $40\mathrm{mg/mL}$ DMSO. The drug stock solution was diluted to $4~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ with a conditioning medium. Solutions with concentrations of 0, 10, 50, 100, 500, 1000, and $1500~{\upmu\mathrm{g/mL}}$ were prepared with $4\ \mathrm{mg/mL}$ diluted drug solution. The media containing BGCL (Zn-MOF) and BGCL (DMAP) were aspirated from the plate. Subsequently, a $100\upmu\mathrm{L}$ working solution of different concentrations was remixed and incubated for 24 and $48\mathrm{h}$ , respectively. A $100~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ medium containing $10\%$ Cell Counting Kit-8 was added, and the solution was further incubated for $^\textrm{\scriptsize1h}$ . The optical absorption of the solution was measured at $450~\mathrm{nm}$ by Microplate Spectrophotometer (Thermo Mul­ tiskan SKY) [35].
## 2.5. Computational methodology
Materials Studio software (Accelrys Inc.) with the DMol3 program was used for the calculation in this study [36,37]. The geometry opti­ mization function was used with the generalized gradient approxima­ tion of Perdew and Wang (PW91). The basis file was 3.5 with a medium integration accuracy, which achieved reliable results for different re­ actions [38,39].
## 3. Results and discussions
## 3.1. Characterization of Zn-MOF
PXRD was used to characterize $\mathtt{Z n-M O F}$ , as shown in Fig. S3. The results indicated the purity of the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF samples. The PXRD data showed that the position of the main peaks of the single crystals did not vary, indicating the stability of Zn-MOF [40,41]. Whereby the frame­ work did not collapse after heating $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ high-temperature for $14\mathrm{{h}}$ .
The TGA data of $Z\mathbf{n}$ -MOF and the PXRD of TG final residual material with Zn-MOF are shown in Fig. S4. The weight loss curve of $Z\mathbf{n}$ -MOF showed that the decomposition of the complex was divided into five stages. The first stage was the removal of free waters. The second stage was the removal of the combined water. In the third-stage, the $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF structure collapsed. The result of PXRD indicated that the final residual material of TG was $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ (PDF#89–0511, Fig. s4b, s4c). Moreover, the good thermal stability of Zn-MOF was demonstrated.
The results of the BET analysis are shown in Fig. S5. The $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption isotherms indicated the mesoporous nature of the framework with a typical type-IV structure. The BET and Langmuir surface areas were 6.4336 and $11.7837~\mathrm{m}^{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ , respectively. Further, the pore size distribution plot (Fig. S6) of the Zn-MOF revealed the presence of the mesopores with an average diameter (4 V/A) of 12.5494 nm, as obtained by the Barrett–Joyner–Halenda method.
The SEM images of Zn-MOF before and after heating for 3 and $14\mathrm{h}$ at
$200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ are shown in Fig. S7. Zn-MOF had a regular structure with a particle size of $1{-}10\upmu\mathrm{m}$ . In Fig. S7d, no changes were noted on the edges and corners of the particles, which confirmed the stability of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF
after heating at $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $14\mathrm{~h~}$ .
The primary property for the application of MOFs in the biomedical field is their stability, which is determined by the properties of the metal ions and organic ligands, such as charge distribution, electronegativity, shape, size, and rigidity [42]. Therefore, the PXRD, TGA, and SEM re­ sults confirmed the stability of $Z\mathbf{n}$ -MOF.
## 3.2. Characterization of BGCL
The BNCFs and BGCL were investigated by FT-IR spectroscopy. BGCL had four new absorption peaks at 2941, 2885, 1733, and $1451~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ , which were not present in the spectra of the BNCFs, as shown in Fig. 3a. The C H stretching vibration at 2941 and $2885\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponded to $\mathrm{CH}_{3^{-}}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{2^{-}}$ , respectively. The stretching vibration of the $\mathsf{C}{=}\mathsf{C}$ bond at $1733~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was ascribed to the absorption peak of PCL. The defor­ mation vibration of $\mathsf{C}\mathrm{~-~}\mathsf{H}$ at $1451~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ corresponded to the $\mathrm{CH}_{2^{-}}$ absorption peak of PCL [1,2]. These results confirm the successful PCL grafting of the BNCFs surface.
The $^1\mathrm{H}$ NMR spectrum of BGCL is shown in Fig. 3c. The chemical shift of the peak at $\delta=4.0$ ppm corresponds to the internal repeating unit $-\mathrm{CH_{2}O-}$ (e) of PCL, and that at $\delta=3.37$ ppm is consistent with the end-group unit $-\mathrm{CH_{2}O H-}$ (e’) of PCL. The chemical shift of the peak at $\delta=2.26$ ppm corresponds to the end base unit $\mathrm{-COCH_{2}-}$ (a’) of PCL. The signal peak of hydrogen (a) at $\delta=1.53$ ppm corresponds to the internal repeat unit $\mathrm{-COCH_{2}-}$ of PCL. The chemical shift at $\delta=1.4$ ppm corresponds to the hydrogen on $-\mathrm{CH}_{2}-$ at b and d, as shown in Fig. 3c, and that at $\delta=1.3$ ppm corresponds to the hydrogen on $-\mathrm{CH}_{2}-$ at c. The signal peaks for the hydrogen on the cellulose glucose unit appeared between $\delta=3.2$ and $5.6\ \mathrm{ppm}$ [1,2].
The $^{13}\mathrm{C}$ NMR spectrum of the BGCL is shown in Fig. 3d. The carbonyl carbon signal in the PCL side chain was located at position f at $\delta=173.0$ ppm, which was a new peak. The signal peaks at $\delta=102.7$ , 80.5, 71.1–75.3, and $63.7\ \mathrm{ppm}$ were ascribed to $\mathrm{C}_{1},\mathrm{C}_{4},\mathrm{C}_{2,3,5},$ and ${\bf C}_{6}$ in the cellulose backbone, respectively. The signal of the carbon atom substituted by PCL on ${\sf C}_{6}$ was at $\delta=60.8\mathrm{ppm}$ , which coincided with the signal of the carbon atom in the $\mathbf{e}^{\prime}$ position. The signal of the adjacent carbon atom $\mathsf{C}_{1}$ substituted by PCL on the hydroxyl group on ${\sf C}_{2}$ was at $\delta$ $=99.8\$ ppm. The aliphatic carbon signals of PCL were at positions of a, a’, b, c, d, e, and $\mathbf{e}^{\prime}$ with chemical shifts of $\delta=32.1$ , 33.6, 25.3, 24.4, 28.2, 60.8, and $63.7\mathrm{ppm}$ , respectively. This indicated that the hydroxyl group on the cellulose bond was partially substituted by the PCL side chain [1]. Thus, the NMR test demonstrated the successful preparation of BGCL.
