The grafting ratio stabilised when the BNCFs-to-ε-CL ([AGU]/[CL]) molar ratio reached 1:60, and minimal changes were observed in the grafting ratio with a further increase in the molar ratio.
The results of the catalytic activity of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF and DMAP are shown in Fig. 3b and Figs. S8–S17. The catalytic effect of $Z\mathbf{n}$ -MOF was better than that of DMAP and $\mathsf{S n}(\mathsf{O c t})_{2}$ under optimal synthesis conditions. Guo et al. [1] confirmed that the catalytic effect of DMAP was stronger than that of $\mathsf{S n}(\mathsf{O c t})_{2}$ . A grafting ratio of 67.04 and $83.80~\%$ was ob­ tained with the cellous-to-ε-CL ([AGU]/[CL]) molar ratio of 1:20, $2\mathrm{wt\%}$ catalyst, and reaction temperature of $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ The optimal conditions in this work were as follows: $2\mathrm{wt\%}$ catalyst, a temperature of $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C},$ , and a time of $^{12\mathrm{{h}}}$ . The grafting ratio of BGCL (Zn-MOF) increased from 1:40 to 1:80 (which were labeled as BGCL40, BGCL50, BGCL60, BGCL70, and BGCL80 respectively), compared to BGCL (DMAP). Both reached the equilibrium at the BNCFs-to-ε-CL ([AGU]/[CL]) molar ratio of 1:60. In addition, the grafting ratio of BGCL (DMAP) was $76.45\%$ , whereas that of BGCL (Zn-MOF) was $84.12\%$ at the same synthesis condition, which was $10.03\%$ higher. When the BNCFs-to-ε-CL ([AGU]/[CL]) molar ratio was varied, the grafting ratio varied slightly. However, the grafting ratio of BGCL (DMAP) under optimal conditions was lower than that of the grafted polymer prepared by Guo et al. [1] because of the easier agglomeration of the BNCFs than the microcrystalline cellulose. Thus, Zn-MOF has a superior catalytic effect compared to literature-reported catalysts of cellulose- $\cdot\mathbf{g}$ -PCL (Table 4).
Fig. 4a shows the PXRD patterns of the BNCFs and BGCL with different ε-CL contents. The diffraction peaks of the BNCFs are at $2\theta=$ $22.6^{\circ}$ , $16.03^{\circ}$ , and $14.82^{\circ}$ , corresponding to the (200), (101), and (−110) diffraction planes [44], respectively. In the diffraction patterns of BGCL, these peaks were not observed. In contrast, a new broad peak at $2\theta=20.3^{\circ}$ was noted, corresponding to the (110) plane of the PCL chain [45]. The disappearance of the characteristic peak of the BNCFs was ascribed to the introduction of the PCL side-chain that replaced the
BNCFs hydroxyl group, thereby weakening their hydrogen bonding BNCFs. Moreover, the disruption of the regularity of the molecular chain of the BNCFs reduced the crystallinity of the cellulose, resulting in the disappearance of the aforementioned crystalline peak [1].
Fig. 4b shows the low melting point of approximately $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and enthalpy of melting of $89\ \mathrm{J/g}$ for PCL. This low melting point of PCL combined with its high price result in its limited use on its own [2]. Meanwhile, BNCFs have a melting peak temperature of approximately $156^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and an enthalpy of melting of $80~\mathrm{J/g}$ . The melting point of BGCL was in the range of $60.50\mathrm{-}156.02^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with a melting peak temperature of $126{\mathrm{-}}139~^{\circ}{\mathrm{C}}$ , which was significantly higher than that of PCL. The cor­ responding enthalpy of melting was $80{-}311\ \mathrm{J/g}$ . The melting peak indicated the sufficient length of the grafted PCL chains to promote crystallization [18]. This effect was more prominent in the BGCL40 sample, which had a higher enthalpy of melting of $311~\mathrm{J/g}$ . Thus, the BNCFs were grafted with long PCL chains. Eriksson [46] reported the melting peaks observed in the cellulose nanocrystal samples grafted with longer PCL chains only [18]. In this study, the melting peaks were observed for all BGCL samples, indicating that the BNCFs were grafted with long PCL lengths.
The thermal stability of BGCL40, BGCL60, BNCFs, and PCL was determined by TGA in a nitrogen atmosphere. The TG spectra (Fig. 4c) of BNCFs exhibited a single-stage decomposition with a weight loss starting at approximately $245^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and a maximum decomposition temperature of approximately $370\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ The weight loss of BGCL40 and BGCL60 at $40{\mathrm{-}}105^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , which started at lower temperatures of approximately 213 and $200^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , respectively, can be ascribed to the water mass loss relative to the BNCFs. The decomposition temperature of pure PCL was in the range of $340{\mathrm{-}}460^{\circ}\mathbf{C}.$ . Two overlapping broad peaks were noted on the derivative thermogravimetry (DTG) curves of BGCL40 and BGCL60. The first decomposition peak can be ascribed to the degradation of the cel­ lulose, and the second peak is attributed to the decomposition of the PCL side chains [2,16]. BGCL40 and BGCL60 compared to PCL and BNCFs, a negligible degradation peak was noted BNCFs at $500{-}600^{\circ}{\mathrm{C}}$ owing to the small amount of residual catalyst Zn-MOF.
The FE-SEM images (Fig. 4d) show the relatively rough surface of the BGCL with a small amount of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF. The shapes and sizes of Zn-MOF were maintained by heating $14\mathrm{~h~}$ at $200^{\circ}\mathsf{C},$ and the BGCL surface was relatively flat with few cracks. Moreover, a small number of white BNCFs can be observed in Fig. 4e and f. The FE-SEM results confirmed the stability of $Z\mathrm{n}$ -MOF after a reaction for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ .
## 3.3. In vitro cytotoxicity
Amphiphilic polymers can be used in nanoscale biosensing, bio­ catalysis, and bioimaging applications. However, amphiphilic polymers can only be used in biological applications if they have good biotoxicity and biocompatibility [1,2]. Thus, the in vitro cytotoxicity activity of
BGCL (Zn-MOF) and BGCL (DMAP) toward Hela cells was studied (Fig. 5 and Table S6). No cytotoxic effects were recognised through the in vitro response of the samples to Hela cells. After $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , the cell viability of BGCL (Zn-MOF) and BGCL (DMAP) was approximately $100\%$ with no significant difference between the two groups. After $^{48\ \mathrm{h},}$ the cell viability of BGCL $(\mathrm{Zn-MOF})$ and BGCL (DMAP) greatly improved to $130\mathrm{-}160\mathrm{~\%~}$ . Moreover, no cytotoxicity was noted at the suspension concentrations of $\mathrm{0{-}1500\ m g/m L}$ . Further, the samples promoted cell growth. The results are superior to the study of Zuppolini et al. which used the $\left[\mathsf{S n}(\mathsf{O c t})_{2}\right]$ as catalyst to synthesis cellulose-graft-poly(ε-cap­ rolact-one) [2]. When the BGCL (Zn-MOF) concentrations were below $1000~\mathrm{mg/mL}$ , the cell viability gradually improved to ${>}9.7~\%$ of that of BGCL (DMAP). As MOFs are used as cardiovascular biomaterials that can promote tendon regeneration and resist bacterial activities with minimal cytotoxicity [44,47–49], the results of the in vitro cytotoxicity test confirm the good biocompatibility of the amphiphilic polymers.
## 3.4. Mechanism
The main mechanism in the reaction of the cellulose-grafted ε-CL is the initiation of ring-opening polymerization of ε-CL to form long chains of PCL aliphatic. Sattayanon and Gümüta [50–52] used a series of organotin complexes and tin salt as the catalysts to initiate the ringopening polymerization of ε-CL and $\upalpha$ -adamycaprolactone. The calcu­ lations of the natural bond orbitals and transition state of the ringopening polymerization were performed to elucidate the coor­ dination–insertion mechanism of the metal complexes through density functional theory. According to the report, the coordination-insertion mechanism of the ring opening of ε-CL by Zn-MOF is shown in Fig. 6b.
Zn-MOF was heated at $200~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $14\mathrm{~h~}$ to remove the free and co­ ordinated water (Fig. 6a). In the initial stage of the reaction, the carbonyl oxygen on $\scriptstyle\varepsilon-\mathbf{CL}$ as the nucleophile, attacked the coordinated zinc ion to form a weak complex. Simultaneously, the electron cloud density distribution around the zinc ion was changed owing to the co­ ordination of $\varepsilon$ -CL. This weakened the $\mathbf{Z}\mathbf{n}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{O}\mathbf{R}_{2}$ coordination bond, and increased the coordination force between the carbonyl oxygen and zinc ion, thereby forming the four-membered ring transition state TS1. The more electronegative $-\mathsf{O R}_{2}$ ligand rotated and attacked the carbonyl carbon atom to form a covalent bond. As the oxygen atom of the acyloxy has a weak interaction with the zinc ion, it formed a stable intermediate Int1. The coordination of the acyloxy oxygen atoms with the zinc ions, and the inductive effect of the oxygen atom on the electron reduced the density of the electron cloud of the acyloxy bond and decreased its stability. Moreover, the central carbon atom became more electroposi­ tive, prompting the shift of the electron cloud of the originally coordi­ nated carbonyl oxygen atom toward the central carbon atom, thereby forming the second transition state TS2. Owing to the uneven distribu­ tion of the electron cloud of the four-coordinate ring in TS2, the acyloxy bond was easily broken after certain amount of energy was provided. Moreover, the initially coordinated bond between the carbonyl oxygen and zinc ion can also be easily broken, and the oxygen atom of the acyloxy formed a more stable covalent bond with the zinc ion, thereby forming the second intermediate Int2. With the addition of ε-CL, longchain PCL was obtained by repeating the aforementioned mechanism.
To verify the coordination-insertion mechanism of the ring-opening polymerization and analyze the catalytic activity of the Zn-MOF, the steady-state configurations without imaginary frequency and electro­ static potential distributions of ε-CL and Zn-MOF were obtained by theoretical calculations. The electrostatic potential distributions ε-CL and Zn-MOF are shown in Fig. 6c and d. The carbonyl oxygen of ε-CL has the most negative potential, whereas the metal ion of Zn-MOF has a more positive potential distribution. This indicates that the ring-opening polymerization reaction is triggered by the coordination of the carbonyl oxygen of ε-CL to the metal centre ions.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, banana cellulose nanofibres (BNCFs) were successfully prepared using waste banana stems, which not only add value to the product but also have environmental benefits. Zn-MOF was synthesized, and a nitrogen-containing heterocyclic compound [5-(1-hydro-imida­ zolyl) isophthalic acid] with notable biological activity was used as the ligand. The primary factor for the application of MOFs in the biomedical field is their stability. PXRD, TG, and SEM analyses showed the stability of Zn-MOF. Zn-MOF was used to catalyze the ε-CL-grafted BNCFs (BGCL) by homogeneous ring-opening polymerization in ionic liquid. BGCL was successfully synthesized through a green approach. The FE-SEM results confirmed the stability of Zn-MOF after a reaction for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ . The banana cellulose nanofibres grafted with poly(ε-caprolactone) reactions follow the coordination-insertion mechanism, which was identified by theo­ retical calculations using the Materials Studio software. Therefore, BGCL is a non-toxic product with wide application prospects in the field of drug delivery, bioimaging, and sensing, among others. Moreover, the Zn-MOF exhibited its potential as a stable and non-toxic catalyst with an excellent catalytic effect. Thus, this study provides a theoretical basis for the application of MOFs as catalysts in polymerization reactions.
